 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+### 2Q Replacement
+
+Replacement is 2Q: a short-term FIFO queue for first-use sheets and a main LRU queue for re-referenced sheets, with the 12 GiB JPEG-data budget split between the two queues.
+
+- A newly rendered sheet is admitted to the short-term queue.
+- When the short-term queue is at its share of the budget, admitting a new sheet evicts the short-term queue's oldest sheet; first-use sheets never evict main-queue sheets.
+- When a sheet in the short-term queue is requested again, it moves to the main queue, where sheets are ordered by recency of use.
+- When the main queue is at its share of the budget, a sheet moving into it evicts the main queue's least-recently-used sheet.
+- A request for a sheet already in the main queue records it as most recently used.
+
+Queue shares are measured in bytes of cached JPEG data. The split is a tunable parameter; the initial split is 25% short-term / 75% main, to be tuned against observed pilot workloads.
+
+This is what the policy buys: sheets from a long sequential screening occupy only short-term space and leave in FIFO order, while a re-referenced comparison sheet can be displaced only by other main-queue use.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Eviction policy | 2Q (short-term FIFO + main LRU, split budget) | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | LRU is the cheapest to maintain and follows editors' recent work, but one long sequential screening evicts the comparison working set. Aged-frequency counts retain repeatedly popular sheets but add counter maintenance and lag a project switch, keeping yesterday's favorites warm. 2Q accepts an extra queue and a tunable size split so one-off screening sheets cannot displace re-referenced comparison sheets. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+
+1. ✅ Eviction policy settled: 2Q (see Decisions & Alternatives).
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. Queue-size split: initial 25% short-term / 75% main; tune against observed pilot workloads.
